{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B050"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the strongest final case because the magic-wavelength lattice is a sharply identifiable physical solution to a central limitation of many-atom clocks, and because that insight became an exceptionally precise, robust, independently reproducible experimental platform. Katori represents the originating lattice-clock concept and Ye the defining experimental and metrological development. The achievement has matured beyond a remarkable demonstration into a durable capability for precision measurement and fundamental tests, while retaining a compact two-person core and a citation that can be kept scientifically exact.",
    "case_against": "The unresolved issue is historical scope. Optical-clock progress depended on a broad community developing transitions, systematic-error control, comparisons, and applications. Before an award, the record must establish that Katori and Ye are sufficient recipients for the invention and defining development of lattice clocks, without allowing the citation to imply credit for the whole optical-clock enterprise.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that lattice clocks combine a magic-wavelength principle, demanding realization, reproducibility, and a credible Katori-Ye attribution, while requiring a citation limited to lattice clocks rather than all optical timekeeping.",
        "response": "I agree with this delimitation and take it to be the decisive condition for retaining B003. Its strength is not simply record-setting clock performance: it is the experimentally controlled removal of a physical limitation that enabled many-atom optical clocks. A narrowly worded citation recognizes that advance without appropriating the indispensable subsequent work of the wider metrology community."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors KPZ universality because it revealed broadly applicable non-equilibrium fluctuation laws and received unusually precise experimental confirmation, while acknowledging an attribution boundary between formulation and confirmation.",
        "response": "I find B013 a profound alternative, especially because detailed experiments make the universality claim a physical result rather than a merely formal construction. I nevertheless retain B003: the prize-defining experimental principle, its realization, and its two-person allocation are presently more delimited. For KPZ, the division between the original framework, subsequent theoretical development, and experimental credit still needs sharper resolution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that the recollision model and quantum theory of high-harmonic generation reveal an unexpected fundamental light-matter mechanism, but identifies a necessary boundary from the attosecond experimental advances recognized in 2023.",
        "response": "The recollision account is an elegant and long-tested fundamental mechanism, and B050 remains a serious alternative. The stated prior-recognition boundary is consequential, however: the committee must be able to distinguish the proposed theoretical achievement cleanly from the recognized experimental attosecond programme. B003 currently permits a more self-contained citation and clearer allocation of credit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone constitute the indispensable conceptual and experimental core for an optical-lattice-clock citation.",
      "How precisely the citation can separate lattice-clock invention and defining development from the broader optical-clock programme."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "Unchanged: as director of WACQT and a superconducting-circuit theorist with long-standing Chalmers connections in quantum technology, I should avoid judging B049. No comparable connection to the B003 laureates is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
